The paper’s decisive point is now clear: it does not supply a hardness theorem. It supplies executable algorithms—Buchberger’s algorithm, a rational standard-basis/syzygy precheck, and a terminating normal form procedure—and reports dramatic expression swell (including a 70-generator example and multi-minute/multi-hour runs). So a Track A claim would be dishonest. The viable direction is Track B: an exact polynomial ideal-membership certificate whose generic coefficient/syzygy computation is large, but whose planted transformation has a short structural inverse. I’m checking the repository harness before committing to that construction.
